# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# Samsung Odyssey 3D (G90XF): the scaler's vendor device, a USB mass-storage device (1603:452a) whose SCSI
# vendor name is "GCREADER"; Linux gives it a /dev/sgN. Only for any2odyssey-scaler, which is experimental
# and off unless asked for (docs/LENS-SWITCH.md, "The scaler's 3D mode"): it lets the person at the seat
# send that device its 3D-mode command. Installed only by the installer's step 6 (install.sh --scaler), or
# by hand.
#
# What it opens is more than that one command: every program of the person at the seat can then send this
# device any SCSI command. What else the device takes is not known here.
#
# The USB id was read from one monitor, and any2odyssey-scaler asks for the same one. A monitor whose
# device has another id gets nothing from either; any2odyssey-scaler names the id it found.
#
# After copying this file: "sudo udevadm control --reload", then unplug and replug the monitor's USB cable
# (or switch the monitor off and on). odysseyvk-linux reports that "udevadm trigger" on this device
# re-initialises the monitor's hub and takes the lens board and the webcam off the bus until then.
#
# Two lines, because udev wants everything a line asks of the devices above (ATTRS, SUBSYSTEMS) to be true
# of one and the same of them: the USB id is the USB device's, the vendor name the SCSI device's. The first
# line notes the id in a property that is kept nowhere (its name begins with a dot), the second asks for it.
SUBSYSTEM=="scsi_generic", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1603", ATTRS{idProduct}=="452a", ENV{.A2O_SCALER_USB}="1"
SUBSYSTEM=="scsi_generic", ENV{.A2O_SCALER_USB}=="1", SUBSYSTEMS=="scsi", ATTRS{vendor}=="GCREADER*", TAG+="uaccess"
